      "id": 1,
      "text": "In 1843, the town of Brenham was renamed after physician Richard Fox Brenham, who died during the Mier Expedition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brenham%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "German Methodists founded Mission Institute in 1883, which later became Blinn College after receiving support from a reverend.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/brenham-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every May, Brenham celebrates its German roots with Maifest, a lively festival echoing the traditional Volksfest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brenham%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1885, Jewish settlers opened one of Texas' first Orthodox synagogues in Brenham, later relocating it to Austin in 2015.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brenham%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1860 construction of the Washington County Railroad turned Brenham into a rail terminus, making it a key distribution point until 1871.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
